{"type": "reveal", "fact_id": "PF2", "text": "I need to share what I confirmed this morning with the business sponsor. The company's priority is exclusive rights for HelioGrid only in stationary grid-scale energy storage — electric-vehicle applications are expressly excluded. On pricing, the approved benchmark is a 6% royalty on net sales plus a $250,000 annual minimum. I haven't yet updated the draft instructions, so this is the first time the team is hearing this."}
```

```json
{"type": "say", "text": "On sublicensing, I'd recommend we require HelioGrid to obtain our prior written consent before granting any sublicense, and critically, every sublicense must also be subject to the same federal compliance obligations — the government-use rights reservation and the U.S.-manufacturing plan requirement must flow down to all sublicensees. We cannot allow sublicensing that would circumvent the grant terms. Additionally, HelioGrid should be required to provide us with a copy of each sublicense agreement and a list of sublicensees upon request, so we can monitor compliance with the federal requirements. I'd also suggest we include a provision that any sublicense that does not comply with the federal grant terms is automatically void — that gives us a backstop if HelioGrid grants a non-compliant sublicense. Beyond that, I don't have additional IP concerns at this stage, provided the field restriction, government-use reservation, and manufacturing plan are all properly drafted into the agreement before it goes out."}
```

```json
{"type": "say", "text": "Nikhil's sublicensing framework makes sense to me commercially. Requiring prior written consent before sublicensing is consistent with how we've handled field-restricted exclusive licenses — it gives us control over who enters the ecosystem and ensures they're operating within the stationary grid-scale energy storage field. The flow-down of federal compliance obligations to sublicensees is essential, and the automatic voiding provision for non-compliant sublicenses is a good backstop. From a commercial standpoint, I'd add one thing: we should also require that any sublicensee's products fall within the licensed field — stationary grid-scale energy storage only — and that HelioGrid remains responsible for ensuring sublicensee compliance with the field restriction and the EV exclusion. We don't want a sublicensee quietly drifting into EV applications through a side door. With that, I'm comfortable with the package as Elena summarized it: exclusive in stationary grid-scale energy storage, EV excluded, subject to government-use rights, U.S.-manufacturing plan required, 6% royalty on net sales, $250,000 annual minimum, prior-consent sublicensing with federal compliance flow-down. I don't have additional commercial concerns at this stage."}
